###  http://www.semanticweb.org/AMISecOnto#Critical
:Critical rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                   :SeverityLevel .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/AMISecOnto#High
:High rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
               :SeverityLevel .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/AMISecOnto#Low
:Low rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :SeverityLevel .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/AMISecOnto#Medium
:Medium rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                 :SeverityLevel .


#################################################################
#    Annotations
#################################################################

<http://rose.com#ObjectAtRisk> rdfs:comment "The object that is exposed to potential damage or loss within a Risk Experience, i.e., the entity whose value could be diminished by a Threat Event. Roughly: the \"thing being protected\" from a purely risk-theoretic standpoint (before any security apparatus is considered)."@en .


<http://rose.com#ProtectedSubject> rdfs:comment "The Agent whose interests benefit from the proper functioning of a Security Mechanism. This is not necessarily the same Agent who designed the mechanism , the government designs public safety policies, and the functioning of such policies satisfies the government's goal in creating them, while also satisfying the goal of the people who want to be safe. Sometimes the Protected Subject coincides with the Security Designer."@en .


<http://rose.com#RiskAssessor> rdfs:comment """An Agent that performs the Risk Assessment relator, estimating likelihoods and impacts associated with Risk Event Types.
Example: An insurance underwriter evaluating a policy applicant."""@en .


<http://rose.com#RiskEnabler> rdfs:comment "An object that plays an ancillary (non-focal) role in a Risk Experience, its dispositions help enable a threat or risk event to occur, but it is not itself the object whose loss is at stake (that role belongs to the Object at Risk)."@en .


<http://rose.com#RiskEvent> rdfs:comment """An event that is the manifestation of (threat) capacities, vulnerabilities, and, sometimes, intentions that inhere in an agent; the event brings about a situation that hurts the goal of a given agent (a Risk Subject). Specializes into Threat Event and Loss Event, where the loss is both a Loss Event and a Threat Event for subsequent losses in a causal chain.
Example: A data breach (Threat Event) that causes regulatory fines (Loss Event)."""@en .


<http://rose.com#RiskEventType> rdfs:comment """AMISecOnto Comment: A type used to classify Risk Events sharing a common pattern of occurrence. Likelihoods are assigned at this level rather than to individual occurrences, because likelihood values can only be assigned to event types, not individual events, since these have already happened.
Example: \"Phishing Email Delivery\" as a type instantiated by each specific phishing event."""@en .


<http://rose.com#RiskExperience> rdfs:comment """The complex event in which a Risk Subject lives through and registers a Risk Event, updating that agent's risk-relevant beliefs. New evidence — e.g., an event actually occurring — updates the probability values and, accordingly, the agent's quantification of risk.
Example: Anna learning her car was in an accident, which revises her assessment of future risk.""" .


<http://rose.com#RiskSubject> rdfs:comment """An Agent whose goals are hurt by Risk Events; may simultaneously be a Protected Subject when a Security Mechanism applies, as in the case of Anna, who is a Protected Subject but also a Risk Subject with respect to her computer.
Example: A company whose customer database is exposed in a breach"""@en .


<http://rose.com#SecurityDesigner> rdfs:comment "The Agent who creates a Security Mechanism, driven by their own intentions/interests in seeing it function correctly. As noted above, the Agent creating a Security Mechanism is not necessarily the one who is protected by its proper functioning, i.e. the Protected Subject."@en .


<http://rose.com#ThreatCapabilityType> rdfs:comment """A type used to classify Threat Capability dispositions that share a common manifestation pattern.
Example: \"Credential Brute-Forcing\" as a capability type shared by many individual attacker tools."""@en .


